1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,142
Every site depends on sensitive values, such as a mail password or a payment API key.

2
00:00:07,142 --> 00:00:11,125
The Secrets module keeps them encrypted, in one list.

3
00:00:11,125 --> 00:00:16,120
In this video, you create, review, replace, delete, and use secrets.

4
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:27,198
Passwords and API keys often end up in configuration files, settings pages, and shared notes.

5
00:00:27,198 --> 00:00:31,582
Each copy can leak, and nothing lists the keys a site uses or

6
00:00:31,582 --> 00:00:33,610
the ones about to expire.

7
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:39,400
The Secrets module gives each value a name and encrypts it before saving.

8
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:44,695
One list shows every secret, its type, where it is kept, and when it expires.

9
00:00:44,695 --> 00:00:47,630
Settings then refer to a secret by name.

10
00:00:51,887 --> 00:00:55,176
The Secrets module is a feature that you turn on.

11
00:00:55,176 --> 00:00:58,227
Open Tools, Features, and search for Secrets.

12
00:00:58,942 --> 00:01:03,912
Select Enable next to Secrets, and a message confirms that the feature was enabled.

13
00:01:04,376 --> 00:01:07,169
Its pages are under Tools, Security:

14
00:01:07,169 --> 00:01:10,494
Secrets, Migrate Secrets, and Secret Stores.

15
00:01:10,494 --> 00:01:12,156
Let's open Secrets.

16
00:01:12,625 --> 00:01:14,309
The list is empty.

17
00:01:14,309 --> 00:01:17,255
Add Secret creates the first secret.

18
00:01:21,478 --> 00:01:23,375
Select Add Secret.

19
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:26,774
A dialog asks for the type of secret:

20
00:01:26,774 --> 00:01:30,648
Text Secret, RSA Key, or X.509 Certificate.

21
00:01:31,105 --> 00:01:34,897
A Text Secret holds a value such as a password, an API key,

22
00:01:34,897 --> 00:01:36,589
or a connection string.

23
00:01:36,589 --> 00:01:41,255
Let's store the password of the mail account that sends this site's email.

24
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:45,174
Select Create under Text Secret, and enter a name.

25
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:50,060
Settings refer to the secret by this name, so it cannot be changed later.

26
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:54,365
The Store field shows Database, where the secret is kept.

27
00:01:54,365 --> 00:01:56,566
The value is a password field:

28
00:01:56,566 --> 00:02:00,234
it is encrypted when saved, and never displayed again.

29
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:04,496
Add a description so others know what the secret is for.

30
00:02:04,496 --> 00:02:08,796
The expiration date is optional and does not disable the secret:

31
00:02:08,796 --> 00:02:11,868
it reminds you to replace the value in time.

32
00:02:12,374 --> 00:02:13,461
Select Save.

33
00:02:13,461 --> 00:02:18,292
A message confirms the creation, and the list shows the secret with badges

34
00:02:18,292 --> 00:02:21,614
for its type, store, expiration, and last update.

35
00:02:25,846 --> 00:02:32,233
An RSA Key secret holds a key pair that Orchard Core generates for you.

36
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:36,463
Select Add Secret, then Create under RSA Key.

37
00:02:36,463 --> 00:02:42,766
Enter a name, choose a key size of 2048, 3072, or 4096 bits, and save.

38
00:02:43,217 --> 00:02:48,375
Once saved, the private key is never shown, and the key pair cannot be changed.

39
00:02:48,375 --> 00:02:52,077
Only the description and expiration date can be edited.

40
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:55,720
An X.509 Certificate secret has two sources.

41
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:01,272
Certificate store refers to a certificate installed on the server, by thumbprint.

42
00:03:01,272 --> 00:03:07,270
PEM-encoded certificate keeps the certificate text itself, with an optional private key.

43
00:03:11,469 --> 00:03:15,557
The Secrets list shows each name, description, and badges.

44
00:03:15,557 --> 00:03:21,499
A warning above the list counts the secrets that have expired or expire within 30 days.

45
00:03:21,955 --> 00:03:26,531
Secrets that have expired, or expire within 30 days, are highlighted.

46
00:03:26,531 --> 00:03:28,849
An expired secret keeps working:

47
00:03:28,849 --> 00:03:32,205
the highlight is a reminder to replace its value.

48
00:03:32,664 --> 00:03:37,293
Type in the search box and press Enter to find secrets by name,

49
00:03:37,293 --> 00:03:39,574
description, type, or store.

50
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:45,761
The Expiration filter shows expired secrets, secrets that expire within 30 days, or

51
00:03:45,761 --> 00:03:47,822
secrets that never expire.

52
00:03:47,822 --> 00:03:51,170
The Type filter shows the secrets of one type.

53
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:55,625
People who can view secrets also see this warning on the admin home

54
00:03:55,625 --> 00:03:57,763
page, with a Review secrets link.

55
00:04:01,986 --> 00:04:06,419
To replace a value, such as a renewed key, select Edit next to the secret.

56
00:04:06,419 --> 00:04:10,521
The Value field is empty, because stored values are never displayed.

57
00:04:10,521 --> 00:04:13,126
Leave it empty to keep the current value.

58
00:04:13,586 --> 00:04:17,503
Enter the replacement value, and set a later expiration date.

59
00:04:17,503 --> 00:04:22,296
Select Save, and this secret is no longer counted in the expiration warning.

60
00:04:26,543 --> 00:04:29,937
To delete a secret, select Delete next to it and confirm.

61
00:04:29,937 --> 00:04:33,976
Features that refer to a deleted secret can no longer read its value,

62
00:04:33,976 --> 00:04:36,023
so check where it is used first.

63
00:04:36,478 --> 00:04:42,121
To delete several secrets at once, select them, open the Actions menu, and choose Delete.

64
00:04:42,121 --> 00:04:46,458
After you confirm, a message reports how many secrets were deleted.

65
00:04:50,656 --> 00:04:54,654
Settings pages that need a password or a key let you pick a

66
00:04:54,654 --> 00:04:57,176
secret instead of typing the value.

67
00:04:57,176 --> 00:05:01,236
Let's open Settings, Communication, Email, and the SMTP tab.

68
00:05:01,693 --> 00:05:06,502
Check Enable SMTP Provider, enter a sender email address and a host name,

69
00:05:06,502 --> 00:05:08,693
and check Require credentials.

70
00:05:08,693 --> 00:05:11,553
The User name and Password fields appear.

71
00:05:12,021 --> 00:05:15,126
The Password field asks where the value is kept.

72
00:05:15,126 --> 00:05:20,416
User-specified value encrypts the password you type, and keeps it with these settings.

73
00:05:20,416 --> 00:05:23,061
Use a secret refers to a secret by name.

74
00:05:23,526 --> 00:05:26,607
Choose Use a secret, and pick it from the list.

75
00:05:26,607 --> 00:05:31,083
If the secret is missing, New secret opens the secret editor in another

76
00:05:31,083 --> 00:05:34,106
tab, and the reload button refreshes the list.

77
00:05:34,563 --> 00:05:35,695
Select Save.

78
00:05:35,695 --> 00:05:40,791
A message confirms the update, and SMTP reads its password from the secret.

79
00:05:40,791 --> 00:05:45,383
Saving with a secret removes any password stored in these settings.

80
00:05:45,837 --> 00:05:52,581
The settings of email, SMS, Google, GitHub, Microsoft, Meta, X (Twitter), OpenID Connect

81
00:05:52,581 --> 00:05:58,177
clients, and Azure AI Search use this same editor for their credentials.

82
00:06:02,377 --> 00:06:04,717
Two permissions control access.

83
00:06:04,717 --> 00:06:10,475
View secrets lists secrets without their values, and Manage secrets also adds, edits,

84
00:06:10,475 --> 00:06:12,372
moves, and deletes them.

85
00:06:12,372 --> 00:06:17,877
Administrators have both; assign them to other roles under Access Control, Roles.

86
00:06:18,627 --> 00:06:21,403
Two more pages are under Tools, Security.

87
00:06:21,403 --> 00:06:27,192
Migrate Secrets moves credentials that settings already hold into secrets, and Secret Stores

88
00:06:27,192 --> 00:06:29,259
moves secrets between stores.

89
00:06:29,259 --> 00:06:31,327
A separate video covers both.

90
00:06:33,327 --> 00:06:35,201
That is the Secrets module:

91
00:06:35,201 --> 00:06:39,916
passwords and API keys kept encrypted, listed in one place, and referenced by

92
00:06:39,916 --> 00:06:41,620
name from your settings.

93
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:43,097
Thanks for watching.

